WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:09.120
Big data, health informatics, and&nbsp;
bioinfomatics, are some of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:16.320
buzz words that you have heard these days.&nbsp;
What is bioinfomatics? Why is it important?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:20.560
And, how can we use bioinfomatics to&nbsp;
solve some of the problems that we face&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:25.280
everyday? These are some of the questions that&nbsp;
we will attempt to address in this lecture.&nbsp;

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:34.320
In 1799 while constructing a fort in Egypt, a&nbsp;
group of French soldiers came across an ancient&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:39.520
Egyptian tablet containing hieroglyphics&nbsp;
on it. What made the discovery this tablet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:45.760
so ground breaking was not just the&nbsp;
fact that it contained hieroglyphics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:53.360
because by this point quite a few stones without&nbsp;
script had already been discovered. It was what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:57.840
came with the hieroglyphics that made the&nbsp;
stone so important to Greek. Because while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:03.200
for over a thousand years no one has been&nbsp;
able to read the ancient Egyptian script,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:08.400
but everyone trained in classical languages could&nbsp;
read Greek. This discovery 40 years later enabled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:13.520
the French scholar to find a way to decode the&nbsp;
meaning of the hieroglyphics, the symbols which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:18.400
have been silent for over a millennium became a&nbsp;
new window into ancient Egyptian life and culture.

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:26.000
Today, we are faced with a challenge and an&nbsp;
opportunity similar to that of the rosetta&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:30.480
stone. It comes from a language which&nbsp;
works in any human language in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:34.400
immense number of volumes written in&nbsp;
it, and that is the language of DNA.

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:42.640
DNA contains the blueprints for every biological&nbsp;
process of every living thing on earth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:47.120
and even a small defect in the genetic code&nbsp;
can cause devastating health consequences.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:51.920
Understanding how genes work, both&nbsp;
individually and with each other is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:55.520
crucial to the development of the twenty&nbsp;
first century personalized medicine.

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:04.240
However, the problem is that even when we&nbsp;
actually have an organism's DNA in front of us,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:08.000
we are not be possible to sift through&nbsp;
it with an army of people to understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:14.480
it. It is often not easy to tell when an&nbsp;
individual gene does. Even more difficult is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:20.000
to tease out the subtle influences that multiple&nbsp;
genes have on each other. But, answering those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:23.120
kinds of questions is foundational to attacking&nbsp;
genetically influenced diseases at the root cause.

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:33.600
Today, new high-throughput technologies bring&nbsp;
unprecedented opportunities for life science&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:40.000
research. They allow us to get new data never seen&nbsp;
before, to study new questions impossible to study&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:46.800
before, and to discover new phenomena unimaginable&nbsp;
before. Now we can sequence not only one person’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:52.800
genome, but also many different people’s genomes.&nbsp;
This allows us to study the genetic differences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:58.160
between different people at genome scale, as well&nbsp;
as many other questions in population genetics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:03.360
At the same time, we can also study the&nbsp;
genetic differences between patients&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:07.840
and normal controls to find the genetic&nbsp;
mutations responsible for some of the diseases.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:15.120
In addition to the human genome, over&nbsp;
1000 trillion base pairs from over 165,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:21.360
species had been sequenced by the end of 2013.&nbsp;
Just reading them would take 30 million years.

00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:30.960
Life science data is not only big in size, but&nbsp;
also growing exponentially. This figure shows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:35.440
the number of nucleotides sequenced and&nbsp;
stored in the Genbank database over year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:42.000
The number of nucleotides had been increasing&nbsp;
exponentially since 1982, doubling every 20&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:48.320
months. What does this mean? It means that&nbsp;
the data deposited into Genbank in the next 20&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:53.200
months from now on is as much as all the data that&nbsp;
have existed in the history of human till today.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.160 --> 00:04:00.240
At the same time, the sequencing cost had been&nbsp;
decreasing every year. The past few years had seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:06.240
even steeper growth of sequencing data. The reason&nbsp;
is that next-generation sequencing technologies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:12.240
had been developed and used more and more widely&nbsp;
in all areas of life sciences. Next-generation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:17.600
sequencing technologies can sequence one person’s&nbsp;
genome in a day with less than 2000 dollars.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:22.720
Just think about what exciting discoveries&nbsp;
these never-seen-before data may contain!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:28.000
Other than sequencing data, other&nbsp;
high-throughput technologies such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:30.531
as proteomic, metabolomic and et cetera,&nbsp;
had also generated a large variety of data.

00:04:30.531 --> 00:04:30.599
Other than sequencing data, other&nbsp;
high-throughput technologies such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.599 --> 00:04:33.200
as proteomic, metabolomic and et cetera,&nbsp;
had also generated a large variety of data.

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:42.000
With great opportunities come great challenges.&nbsp;
The huge amount of data requires efficient&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:49.200
methods. Exponential growth requires scalable&nbsp;
methods. The low signal-to-noise ratio requires&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:56.240
accurate methods. And handling multiple types&nbsp;
of data requires data integrative methods. These&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.240 --> 00:05:02.320
are significant technical challenges. But,&nbsp;
it depends on how you look at it. Technical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.320 --> 00:05:07.200
challenges can also mean opportunities&nbsp;
for technical innovations. The birth and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:11.920
growth of the field of bioinfomatics has been&nbsp;
driven by these opportunities and challenges.

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:19.920
As you may be able to realize by now, the&nbsp;
integration between life science and computer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:24.880
science is inevitable, and the result is the&nbsp;
birth and growth of the field of bioinfomatics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:29.840
Bioinfomatics can be defined as an&nbsp;
interdisciplinary field that develops&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:34.480
and applies computer and computational&nbsp;
technologies to study biomedical questions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:41.920
It has two roles. As a technology, bioinfomatics&nbsp;
is a powerful technology to manage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:44.720
search, and analyze big data in life sciences.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.760 --> 00:05:51.920
As a methodology, bioinfomatics is a top-down&nbsp;
holistic, data-driven, genome-wide, and systems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.920 --> 00:05:57.360
approach that generates new hypotheses, finds new&nbsp;
patterns, and discovers new functional elements.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:03.680
It complements traditional experimental&nbsp;
biology methods. A seamless combination&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:08.720
of computational and experimental methods should&nbsp;
be the best way to study a biological question.

00:06:11.920 --> 00:06:18.240
Bioinformatics is truly interdisciplinary.&nbsp;
It studies questions in biology and medicine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:23.600
while developing and applying methods in computer&nbsp;
sciences, mathematics, statistics, and physics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:30.000
It overlaps with medical and clinical informatics,&nbsp;
systems biology, and synthetic biology.

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:38.640
The Bio in bioinformatics signifies the biological&nbsp;
questions it studies, many of which can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:43.280
grouped under the conceptual framework from&nbsp;
genotype to phenotype and the Central Dogma.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:49.360
Some examples may help you understand&nbsp;
better. For instance, sequence alignment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.240 --> 00:06:56.480
Are the sequences of two genes or proteins&nbsp;
similar? Could they be homologous? How can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.480 --> 00:07:00.720
we find the closest homologue of the gene&nbsp;
we are studying from the vast databases?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.600 --> 00:07:05.840
Can I use the known function of a known gene&nbsp;
to guide the study of my gene of interest?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:13.200
Given DNA and genome sequences, how can we find&nbsp;
the genes in the vast genome? How can we compare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:18.320
the similarity and difference between two whole&nbsp;
genomes and reconstruct the evolutionary history?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:24.800
What are the syntenic regions between two&nbsp;
genomes? How can we identify which genomic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:30.640
regions are methylated? At the level of RNA&nbsp;
expression, we often need to find out which genes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.640 --> 00:07:35.680
are differentially expressed between two organs&nbsp;
or tissues, between tumor and normal tissues,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:41.600
or between two different developmental stages?&nbsp;
At the level of proteins, how do you identify&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.600 --> 00:07:47.600
the expressed proteins from mass spec data?&nbsp;
Proteins do not exist in a linear form in nature.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.640 --> 00:07:52.560
Instead, they are folded into beautiful&nbsp;
three dimensional structures in nature.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.520 --> 00:07:57.840
How can we predict the three-dimensional&nbsp;
structure of a protein from its sequence?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.480 --> 00:08:04.800
Molecules do not exist or function in isolation.&nbsp;
Instead, they form complex molecular network.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.760 --> 00:08:11.200
How can we construct protein-protein interaction&nbsp;
network, transcription regulation networks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.200 --> 00:08:16.640
and metabolic and signaling pathways? What&nbsp;
are the dynamic features of these networks?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.680 --> 00:08:22.000
Furthermore, with these data and researches,&nbsp;
how can we simulate a virtual cell?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:28.160
Last but not least, how do you compare different&nbsp;
people’s genomes and use population genetic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.160 --> 00:08:33.040
approaches to compare the genomes of the ill&nbsp;
and the healthy, and identify the gene mutations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.040 --> 00:08:38.320
that cause diseases? From these examples, we&nbsp;
can see that there are many interesting and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.320 --> 00:08:42.640
important questions in life sciences that&nbsp;
await our bioinformatics investigation.

00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:46.880
Seeing bioinformatics from another angle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.880 --> 00:08:52.240
the informatics in Bioinformatics signifies the&nbsp;
information and computational methods to manage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.240 --> 00:08:59.120
search, and analyze the data. The theme runs&nbsp;
along the axis from data to discovery. First,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.120 --> 00:09:04.800
the storage, index, and search of terabytes to&nbsp;
petabytes of big data require advanced databases.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.760 --> 00:09:09.760
Just imagine, your laptop probably&nbsp;
has several Gigabytes of hard disk.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.720 --> 00:09:15.520
However just one set of our experiments often&nbsp;
generates one or more terabytes of data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.520 --> 00:09:22.000
which is too large to fit in your laptop. Managing&nbsp;
such big data requires advanced database systems.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.040 --> 00:09:27.360
Analysis of noisy big data requires&nbsp;
the development of many algorithms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.360 --> 00:09:33.280
software tools, and web servers. These make&nbsp;
up a big portion of bioinformatics research.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.160 --> 00:09:38.880
A tradition in bioinformatics since the&nbsp;
early days till today has been to make most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.880 --> 00:09:44.320
algorithms and software open source. This&nbsp;
has contributed significantly to the broader&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.320 --> 00:09:50.720
life science research, and had helped to advance&nbsp;
bioinformatics itself as well. With these tools,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.720 --> 00:09:55.120
we can do a lot of data mining to discover&nbsp;
new patterns and phenomena in life science.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.160 --> 00:10:01.760
Finally, by integration of data and tools, we can&nbsp;
build predictive models of biological systems.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.720 --> 00:10:07.360
As more and more high-throughput technologies&nbsp;
are used more and more in life science, there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.360 --> 00:10:12.240
will continue to be numerous new opportunities&nbsp;
for technology innovation in bioinformatics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:16.960
To meet this challenge, a new breed of&nbsp;
professional that could collaborate and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.960 --> 00:10:21.760
communicate with biomedical researchers,&nbsp;
clinicians, quantitative scientists and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.760 --> 00:10:26.400
bioethicists is needed to translate the&nbsp;
genomic knowledge to improve patient outcomes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.360 --> 00:10:41.520
In summary, bioinformatics is central&nbsp;
to molecular life-science research.

